To solve the system of congruences, we start with the given equations:
\[
\begin{align*}
x + 3y & \equiv 7 \pmod{n} \quad \text{(1)} \\
2x + 2y & \equiv 18 \pmod{n} \quad \text{(2)} \\
3x + y & \equiv 7 \pmod{n} \quad \text{(3)}
\end{align*}
\]

First, let's simplify equation (2):
\[
2x + 2y \equiv 18 \pmod{n}
\]
We can divide both sides by 2 (since \( n > 18 \) and \( n \) is a positive integer, 2 has an inverse modulo \( n \)):
\[
x + y \equiv 9 \pmod{n} \quad \text{(4)}
\]

Now we have:
\[
\begin{align*}
x + 3y & \equiv 7 \pmod{n} \quad \text{(1)} \\
x + y & \equiv 9 \pmod{n} \quad \text{(4)} \\
3x + y & \equiv 7 \pmod{n} \quad \text{(3)}
\end{align*}
\]

Next, subtract equation (4) from equation (1):
\[
(x + 3y) - (x + y) \equiv 7 - 9 \pmod{n}
\]
\[
2y \equiv -2 \pmod{n}
\]
\[
2y \equiv n - 2 \pmod{n} \quad \text{(5)}
\]

Next, subtract equation (4) from equation (3):
\[
(3x + y) - (x + y) \equiv 7 - 9 \pmod{n}
\]
\[
2x \equiv -2 \pmod{n}
\]
\[
2x \equiv n - 2 \pmod{n} \quad \text{(6)}
\]

From equation (6), we need to find the smallest nonnegative integer \( a \) such that:
\[
2x \equiv a \pmod{n}
\]
Clearly, \( a = n - 2 \).

Thus, the smallest nonnegative integer \( a \) is:
\[
\boxed{n-2}
\]

